On Feb 5, 2:11 pm, ais523 <ais...@nethack4.org> wrote:
> Emacs' shell is not good as a shell,

Actually, there's more than one way to run a
shell in Emacs, and they have different strengths
and weaknesses.  Most folks run a stock shell
(e.g., bash) in shell-mode.  That works, and it
has a few advantages (over running a shell in
another non-Emacs window), but it's basically
just a normal shell.

The other major option is eshell, which is
much more _integrated_ with Emacs and
has a lot of additional advantages, but it
also has differences and thus a learning
curve.  It's really only useful if you are
fairly comfortable with elisp already.

> but it's better than the stock one
> that comes with Windows.

Hey, modern versions of Windows come with
cmd.exe, which is WAY better than the command
shell we used to get with Windows back when I
used Windows on a regular basis.

(There's also PowerShell, but I've never taken
the time to learn it.  It's supposed to be pretty
good, from what I've read, but it's got a learning
curve that would make TeX jealous.)


